Consider the second 0⁺ excited state of the ¹²C nucleus (the so-called Hoyle state), located approximately 7.65 MeV above the ground state and just above the 3α breakup threshold. Modern ab initio nuclear-structure approaches — including the no-core Monte Carlo shell model, the symmetry-adapted no-core shell model, and nuclear lattice effective field theory based on chiral interactions evaluated by quantum Monte Carlo methods — have reproduced parts of the low-lying ¹²C spectrum and, in some cases, the excitation energy and monopole transition matrix element M(E0; 0⁺₁ → 0⁺₂) of the Hoyle state.

The problem: within each of these ab initio frameworks, compute, for the 0⁺₂ Hoyle state of ¹²C from first-principles nucleon dynamics (without phenomenological cluster assumptions), the following observables and demonstrate quantitative agreement with experiment:
(a) the inelastic electron-scattering form factor F(q) for the 0⁺₁ → 0⁺₂ transition as a function of momentum transfer q (in particular reproducing the experimentally observed diffraction structure and magnitude);
(b) the monopole transition probability M(E0; 0⁺₁ → 0⁺₂) (equivalently the strength extracted from F(q) at low q);
(c) the root-mean-square matter (or charge) radius of the Hoyle state, which experimentally is significantly larger than that of the ¹²C ground state.

Establish whether each of the listed ab initio methods can simultaneously reproduce (a), (b), and (c), thereby validating it as a correct microscopic theory of the Hoyle state, rather than reproducing only the excitation energy or partial spectroscopic information.

